course: biology
lesson: 283
title: Wolves in Yellowstone, beavers in Britain and bringing nature back
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: By the 1920s every wolf in Yellowstone Park in America had been killed, and without them the elk grew bold, stood by the rivers and munched the young willow and aspen trees.
explain: In 1995 and 1996, 31 wolves were brought back from Canada; the elk had to keep moving again, and in some places young trees could grow, which helped beavers and birds.
explain: A change at the top of a food chain that ripples all the way down is called a trophic cascade, like knocking over the first domino in a row.
explain: The famous video says the wolves «changed the rivers», but scientists say the story is more complicated, because bears, people and weather mattered too; still, the main lesson holds: top predators shape the whole web.
explain: Bringing missing animals back and letting nature run itself again is called rewilding, and in Britain it has brought back beavers, white storks and nightingales.
fact: Wolves were killed out of Yellowstone by the 1920s, and 31 wolves were brought back from Canada in 1995 and 1996; without wolves, elk ate young trees along the rivers, and with wolves back, elk moved more and some trees could grow again.
fact: Wolves helped willows and aspens in some places, which helped beavers and birds; scientists say the story is more complicated than «wolves changed the rivers», because bears, people and weather also mattered.
fact: A trophic cascade is a change at the top of a food chain that ripples down: wolves eat elk, elk eat trees, and trees shade streams and feed beavers.
fact: Sea otters, sea urchins and kelp are another trophic cascade.
fact: Wolves sometimes kill sheep and cattle near parks; farmers are paid for losses, and guard dogs, fences and lights help keep wolves away, so that farmers and wild animals can live side by side.
fact: Beavers were hunted out of Britain about 400 years ago and have been returning; official releases into the wild in England were reported from 2025.
fact: Beaver dams slow floods, clean water and make ponds full of life, though some farmers worry about flooded fields.
fact: Lynx are shy wild cats that eat deer and are not known to attack people; some people want them back in Scotland, some farmers fear for their sheep, and it is debated carefully.
fact: Rewilding means letting nature run itself again, often by returning missing animals; at Knepp in England a farm was left to go wild, and storks, turtle doves and nightingales came back.
fact: White storks nested in England again in 2020 for the first time in hundreds of years, after birds were bred and released; in many countries storks on rooftops are a sign of good luck.
fact: Wild wolves are shy, and attacks on people are very rare; old stories like Little Red Riding Hood come from times when people feared wolves and lost livestock.
fact: Wolves howl to call their pack and warn other packs, at any time of night or day; they point their heads up because the sound carries further, and the moon has nothing to do with it.
fact: The idea of a fierce «alpha wolf» fighting for the top came from studies of unrelated wolves kept together in zoos; in the wild a pack is a family led by the mum and dad, and the scientist who made the word popular later asked people to stop using it.
exercise: When were wolves brought back to Yellowstone? | In 1995 and 1996 | In 1920 | In 1872 | In 2019 and 2020
exercise: Where did the Yellowstone wolves come from? | Canada | Russia | Scotland | Mexico
exercise: What did elk do in Yellowstone when there were no wolves? | Stood by the rivers and ate young trees | Left the park for ever and never came back | Started hunting beavers | Stopped eating altogether
exercise: What is a trophic cascade? | A change at the top of a food chain that ripples down | A tall waterfall in a national park, like Yellowstone's own | A kind of wolf howl | A row of beaver dams
exercise: What do scientists say about the story that wolves «changed the rivers»? | It is more complicated: bears, people and weather mattered too | It is completely true in every detail | It is a made-up fairy tale | The rivers changed the wolves instead
exercise: Which is another trophic cascade? | Sea otters, sea urchins and kelp | Cats, dogs and mice in a cartoon | Clouds, rain and rainbows | Cars, roads and petrol
exercise: How are farmers helped when wolves kill their sheep? | They are paid for losses and use guard dogs, fences and lights | They are given new wolves of their own to keep and train as sheepdogs | They are told to move house | Nothing at all is done
exercise: When were beavers hunted out of Britain? | About 400 years ago | About 40 years ago | About 4,000 years ago | Last year
exercise: What good do beaver dams do? | Slow floods, clean water and make ponds full of life | Make rivers run faster and straighter to the sea | Dry out the land completely | Keep all the fish out of rivers
exercise: What do lynx mainly eat? | Deer | People | Grass | Fish only
exercise: What is rewilding? | Letting nature run itself again, often by returning missing animals | Building zoos in cities | Planting gardens full of plastic flowers that never need watering | Teaching wild animals tricks
exercise: Which birds came back at Knepp in England? | Storks, turtle doves and nightingales | Penguins and puffins | Parrots, flamingos and peacocks from India | Ostriches and emus
exercise: When did white storks nest in England again for the first time in hundreds of years? | In 2020 | In 1820 | In 1066 | In 1995
exercise: How often do wild wolves attack people? | Very rarely | Every day | Every full moon | Only on Sundays
exercise: Why do wolves howl? | To call their pack and warn other packs | To talk to the moon | Because they are always sad and lonely at night | To scare the Sun away
exercise: What is a real wild wolf pack? | A family led by the mum and dad | A gang of strangers fighting to be boss | One alpha wolf and its servants | A group of wolves from different zoos
test: In Little Red Riding Hood the wolf eats grandma. What is true about real wolves? | Wild wolves are shy and attacks on people are very rare | Wolves often dress up as people | Wolves hunt grandmothers in forests, just as the old stories say | Wolves eat people whole
test: In cartoons a wolf always howls at a big full moon. What is right? | Wolves howl to call their pack at any time, and the moon has nothing to do with it | Wolves can only howl at a full moon | Wolves howl because the bright light of the full moon hurts their eyes at night | Wolves howl to make the moon rise
test: «my brother says he's the alpha wolf of the house» Where did the idea of the alpha wolf come from? | Zoo studies of unrelated wolves; wild packs are families led by mum and dad | Studies of wild wolf families for hundreds of years | A wolf that won a fight with a bear | Old Roman wolf laws
test: Count them: a wolf family has mum, dad and 4 cubs. How many legs, ears and tails are there? | 24 legs, 12 ears and 6 tails | 24 legs, 6 ears and 6 tails | 16 legs, 12 ears and 6 tails | 24 legs, 12 ears and 4 tails
test: What would happen to young willow trees by a river if all the wolves were taken away again? | Elk would graze there more and many young trees might be eaten | The trees would grow faster | The trees would turn into aspens | The rivers would dry up the very next day and the trees would all die
test: «why would anyone want wolves back, they just eat stuff» What is the best answer? | Top predators shape the whole food web, helping trees, beavers and birds | Wolves are only wanted so that tourists can take photos of them for their friends | Wolves eat only grass | They do not matter at all
test: «a beaver's back in our river, it'll ruin everything» What is fairest? | Beaver dams slow floods and make ponds full of life, but some farmers worry about flooded fields | Beavers always destroy towns | Beavers are dangerous to children | Beavers bring nothing but floods
test: «lynx in Scotland?? they'll eat kids» What is right? | Lynx are shy, eat deer and are not known to attack people | Yes, lynx hunt children | Lynx are as big as lions and hunt anything they can find | Lynx eat only sheep
test: Count them: a stork pair on a chimney has 3 chicks. How many legs and wings are there in the nest? | 10 legs and 10 wings | 6 legs and 6 wings | 10 legs and 5 wings | 20 legs and 10 wings
test: «they said the wolves changed the rivers, so wolves dig rivers?» What really happened? | Wolves changed how elk moved, which helped trees in some places, and that helped beavers and birds | Wolves dug new rivers with their paws | Wolves drank the rivers dry | Wolves carried stones to make dams
test: A farm that was hard to farm is left wild. What might happen, as at Knepp? | Missing birds like nightingales and turtle doves might come back | All the animals would leave | It would turn into a desert | It would fill up with lions
test: «why'd they pay the farmer, the wolf ate his sheep not them» What is the reason? | Fair rules help farmers and wild animals live side by side | Farmers are paid to feed wolves | The farmer sold the sheep to the wolf | It is a reward for every wolf the farmer shoots on his land
exam: Identify the phenomenon whereby a change in the population of a top predator affects successive levels of a food chain. | A trophic cascade | Natural selection | Metamorphosis | Hibernation
exam: State the number of wolves reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 and 1996, and the country of their origin. | Thirty-one, from Canada | Thirteen, from Mexico | Three hundred and ten, from Russia | Thirty-one, from Scotland
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the howling of wolves. | It serves to summon the pack and to warn other packs, and bears no relation to the moon | It is performed exclusively upon nights of the full moon | It is directed at the moon by reason of the light it emits | It signifies that the wolf is in pain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the structure of a wild wolf pack? | A family led by the parents | An assembly of unrelated wolves under a single dominant male | A temporary gathering for the duration of winter | A group composed exclusively of male wolves
